---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import TaxDragLab from "../../components/TaxDragLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import RegionNote from "../../components/RegionNote.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="The Tax Drag: What a Taxable Account Costs"
  description="Tax on a taxable account isn't a one-off haircut — it's a leak that compounds. Watch the gap between a sheltered and a taxable account widen year by year, and see exactly where the drag comes from."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>The tax drag, over time</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Hold the same investment in a tax-sheltered account and in a taxable one,
      and the two don't just finish apart — they <em>drift</em> apart, a little
      more every year. Every dollar paid in tax along the way is a dollar that
      never compounds again. Watch the gap open up, then see what actually
      causes it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container">
    <RegionNote>
      US-focused — this tool assumes US tax treatment: ordinary-income vs.
      long-term capital-gains rates, and a taxable brokerage alongside a
      tax-sheltered account. The drag exists in any taxed account anywhere; the
      specific rates and account types shown are American.
    </RegionNote>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <TaxDragLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why the drag compounds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Tax paid along the way never compounds.</strong> Dividends and
          the gains a fund distributes are taxed the year they happen, even if you
          reinvest. That's money pulled out of the compounding machine forever, so
          the shortfall grows faster than the tax itself.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Turnover is the villain.</strong> A fund that trades a lot is
          forced to realize and hand you gains every year, much of it short-term
          and taxed at your ordinary rate. A broad index fund barely turns over,
          so it defers almost everything. This is a big, underrated reason indexing
          wins after tax.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Deferral is a superpower.</strong> The capital-gains tax you owe
          at the end is often the smallest slice, because it waited decades before
          coming due, letting the whole balance compound in the meantime. Not
          selling is itself a tax strategy.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This models a single lump sum with no state taxes or tax-loss harvesting,
        to keep the mechanism clear. It's the case for sheltering what you can and
        holding tax-efficient funds where you can't. Educational only, not
        financial advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="tax-drag" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/tax-drag" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro { padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md); max-width: var(--measure-wide); }
  .lede { font-size: var(--step-1); color: var(--color-text-soft); line-height: 1.5; }
  .wl-explainer { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
</style>
